ontent on Alcohol Cravings\n\nDescription:\nResearch shows that most adults over the age of 18 drink alcohol and almost 25% of these adults indicate they are binge-drinkers (NIAA, 2016). Freeman et al. (2010) suggests that viewing pictures of alcohol promotes social disinhibition. The presence of alcoholic drinks in films is linked to an increase in alcohol consumption and risky behavior among young adults (Wills et al., 2009). Litt and Stock (2011) showed that viewing content showcasing alcohol on social media leads to more liberal norms about alcohol. The current study seeks to examine the impact of manipulated social media posts on adults' perceptions and social norms about alcoholic drinks. Participants were exposed to experimenter-created social media posts, and then completed surveys; they were randomly assigned to a celebrity or non-celebrity post with or without alcohol. Results showed that celebrity posts did not seem to impact alcohol cravings in young adults. Overall males reported higher cravings for alcohol than females. Further research in this area should include different manipulations of alcohol brands to test these effects. For example, manipulations of different brands of alcohol versus showing alcohol in a glass to test for the effects of different visual cues.\n\nEvaluate whether this book satisfies the user's search intent.\n\nReturn:\n\n* score (0, 1, or 2)\n* concise reason\n", 'role': 'user'}], 'model': 'gpt-4o-mini', 'response_format': {'type': 'json_schema', 'json_schema': {'schema': {'description': 'Structured relevance judgment for a single (query, book) pair.\n\nThe ``reason`` field is mandatory so every judgment can be audited\nor used for fine-tuning the judge model later.', 'properties': {'score': {'description': 'Relevance score: 0 = not relevant, 1 = somewhat relevant, 2 = highly relevant.', 'title': 'Score', 'type': 'integer'}, 'reason': {'description': 'Concise explanation (1-3 sentences) justifying the score. Used for auditing a